<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	The thumbnail extension's sandbox: the same grants as the preview's.

	com.apple.security.app-sandbox
	com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only
	  Required for the same reasons as in MBRPreview.entitlements: PlugInKit
	  loads no unsandboxed extension, and the second is what lets the
	  extension consume QuickLook's sandbox extension for the file whose
	  thumbnail it was asked for.

	com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-only
	  Also as in the preview, and for the same files: a page's images, the
	  frontmatter cover image, and .mbr/theme.css, .mbr/user.css and
	  .mbr/config.toml at the repository root all live outside the one-file
	  grant. MBRPreview.entitlements explains why the grant is "/" rather than
	  home-relative.

	  What keeps a note from reading anything with it is in code. Rust
	  (src/quicklook.rs) is the only thing that turns note content into a path,
	  and it hands over only paths it has proven are regular files inside the
	  note's repository: the attachment list (collect_preview_attachments) and
	  the cover image (thumbnail_cover_image). The page itself can name no
	  file. Its cid: URLs are answered by AttachmentSchemeHandler from that list
	  alone, and file: URLs are blocked outright by the content rule list in
	  WebSnapshotter.swift.

	com.apple.security.network.client
	  Not for the page: a thumbnail still loads no remote content. It is here
	  because WebKit will not run without it. A sandboxed WKWebView's helper
	  processes check the host's entitlements, and without this key the GPU
	  process exits the moment it starts:

	    com.apple.WebKit.GPU: Application does not have permission to
	    communicate with network resources.

	  - so every snapshot failed in Finder and the generic icon was shown.
	  An unsandboxed test harness never sees this; only the real extension
	  does.

	  Remote content is kept out by the content rule list in
	  WebSnapshotter.swift instead ("mbr-thumbnail-no-network"), which blocks
	  every load that is not cid:, data:, about: or blob:, http(s) and file:
	  included. That rule is now the only barrier, and it is what keeps a
	  thumbnail from waiting on a remote server (a remote image would hold the
	  page's load event, and so the snapshot, multiplied by every note in a
	  Finder window) or caching content that later changes.
	  ThumbnailProviderTests pins that it compiles and blocks remote loads.

	Like the preview's file, this is the only source of these keys for the Nix
	build, which signs with exactly what is written here. flake.nix's
	postFixup verifies all three keys are present on the signed appex.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-only</key>
	<array>
		<string>/</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
